<div class="meta">Lesson 0023 · Systems design · from PR #94 (#91, #93)</div>
<h1>The Single Authoritative Source, and the Moonwalk Bug</h1>
<p>Two of this milestone's fixes look unrelated — one is about where a bullet
goes, the other about when a preview figure spins. They are the same lesson.
Both are cases where a value was <em>reconstructed</em> next to its real
source instead of <em>derived</em> from it, and the copy drifted.</p>

<h2>The bug class: the moonwalk</h2>
<p>The name comes from an earlier fix (#61): a character whose facing was
computed in one place and whose movement was computed in another, so it slid
backwards while appearing to walk forward — a moonwalk. The shape recurs
whenever two consumers each derive "the same" quantity from raw inputs
separately. They agree until one side changes its math, and then they
silently disagree.</p>

<h2>Case 1 — aim reconstructed from feet (#91)</h2>
<p>The camera looked along yaw <em>and</em> pitch from eye height. The weapon
built its own aim: yaw only, flat, spawned from the player's feet. Fire while
looking up and the round left horizontally from the ground — the crosshair
and the bullet were two different vectors that happened to share a yaw.</p>
<p>The fix is not "add pitch to the weapon too". That just makes two copies
that agree <em>today</em>. The fix is one function both sides read:</p>
<pre><code>// src/core/Math.hpp
inline Vec3 lookDirection(float yawDeg, float pitchDeg) {
    const float yr = degToRad(yawDeg), pr = degToRad(pitchDeg);
    const float cp = std::cos(pr);
    return normalize({std::sin(yr)*cp, std::sin(pr), std::cos(yr)*cp});
}</code></pre>
<pre><code>// the camera  (RaylibRenderer.cpp:141)
const Vec3 dir = lookDirection(pose.yawDeg, pose.pitchDeg);
// the weapon  (main.cpp:567)
world.playerAttack(lookDirection(player.yawDeg, player.pitchDeg));</code></pre>
<p>Now the aim <em>cannot</em> drift from the crosshair, because there is no
second aim to drift. A weapon added next year gets correct behavior for free
— the bug is designed out, not patched over. Note <code>kEyeHeight</code> is
shared the same way: camera and spawn origin both read the one constant.</p>

<h2>Case 2 — a decision re-sampled every frame (#93)</h2>
<p>The creator preview rotates when you drag it, but a click on a control
button must not. The first cut asked each frame: "is the cursor over an
interactive rect right now?" That re-derives the intent — drag vs click —
from a value (cursor position) that moves <em>during</em> the very gesture it
is trying to classify. A control-click flicked off its button mid-press spun
the preview; a real drag that grazed a button stalled.</p>
<p>The intent is fixed at one instant — the press — so read it once and hold
it:</p>
<pre><code>if (IsMouseButtonPressed(MOUSE_BUTTON_LEFT))
    previewDragging = !menu.pointOverInteractive(GetMousePosition()); // latch
if (!IsMouseButtonDown(MOUSE_BUTTON_LEFT))
    previewDragging = false;                                          // release</code></pre>
<p>This is the same move as #91 in the time dimension: the authoritative fact
("this gesture is a drag") is established once, at its source event, and every
later frame reads that latch instead of recomputing an answer from data that
has since moved on.</p>

<h2>Deriving toward a target: <code>shortestAngleDelta</code></h2>
<p>When the preview is idle it eases back to front. "Face the camera" is
derived every frame from one owned value (<code>player.yawDeg + 180</code>),
never stored as a second copy that could go stale:</p>
<pre><code>const float defaultYaw = player.yawDeg + 180.f;   // derived, not cached
previewYaw += shortestAngleDelta(previewYaw, defaultYaw)
            * (1.f - std::exp(-kPreviewEaseRate * dt));</code></pre>
<p><code>shortestAngleDelta</code> wraps the difference to [-180, 180] so the
ease always takes the short way round instead of unwinding through 359°.</p>

<h2>How to recognise it before it ships</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Smell</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
<tr><td>Two sites compute "the same" value from raw inputs</td><td>Extract one function; both call it</td></tr>
<tr><td>A per-frame test answers a question fixed at an earlier event</td><td>Latch the answer at that event; read the latch</td></tr>
<tr><td>A value is cached "for convenience" beside its owner</td><td>Derive it on read from the owner</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="callout good"><strong>The general shape.</strong> A quantity used
in two places should have exactly one place that computes it — a shared
function (space) or a latched event (time). The moment a second reconstruction
exists, the two are only ever <em>coincidentally</em> equal, and coincidences
expire.</div>

<div class="callout warn"><strong>The tell in review.</strong> If a bug fix
adds a term to a copy so it matches the original "now", stop — you have made
two copies agree, not made them one. Ask where the value is truly owned and
have the other site derive from there.</div>
